Date: Fri, 16 Jul 1993 18:26:44 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: What are big upcoming problems?

Rich Walker:
> My current `big problem' I like to think about is the first 15 years of
> _real_ robotics....  I suspect the scenario involves something like 
> 90% of the current workforce in most countries being replaced by ma
> machines.

I tend to doubt this.  Automation of intellectual tasks (especially
subsets of management, math, engineering, and science) seems to be 
easier than automation of many kinds of blue-collar work.  Right now, 
computer networks and fax machines are flattening organizations and 
decimating middle management.  Next, we will likely see the
elimination of most in-house engineering as lean, CAD-heavy
consulting services take over.  Scientists will demand high
pay only as long as government continues major subsidies of
obsolete methods of doing science.  We'll keep our jobs
only if we leverage orders-of-magnitude productivity gains
into orders-of-magnitude increases in the value of our
products.  That means coming out with a vast new suite
of valuable products, many of them in the medical field
(medicine is the only insatiable necessity, assuming the
government doesn't artificially limit the size of the market).

Robots will take over only slowly, and first in the newer industries
(eg high-speed electronic assembly).  The major impact on blue collar 
workers will be the continual shift of manufacturing to the 3rd world, 
with residual protected and hobby capabilities remaining at home.  Blue
collar workers have been a dwindling part of the economy for
a long time now, and that will continue, but there won't
be any major revolution -- the remaining job skills are too
diverse to automate in one fell swoop, and the fraction of
the economy that's blue collar labor costs is far too low.
Massive automation of the service industries would have more
impact, but "services" cover an even wider variety of
tasks, from the purely intellectual to the purely servile.

In short, the biggest "future shock" will happen right at
home, our own science and engineering jobs.   We have
at least as much adapting to do as the rest of the economy,
probably more.  Don't waste too many tears over blue collar
workers when our own heads are on the chopping block!

