I had an extended debate about this with Tim Freeman on Cryonet a long time
ago, which you can get from the Cryonet archives via e-mail if you are
sufficiently motivated.  Basically, my points were these:  the bulk of
human usefulness is about 90% domain knowledge, and only about 10%
interchangeable manual skills that basically anybody (including near-future
robots) can learn; the *really* valuable human skills are
cognitive/conceptual ones which robots will not be able to duplicate until
very late in the game, if ever; it is more parsimonious to assume that we
will be able to augment human intelligence, which we observe to already
exist, than that we will be able to create superhuman AIs, which no one has
done yet; so overall we don't have to worry about robots crowding humans
out of any economic or evolutionary niches for quite some time and without
(dare I say) superhuman effort on our part to bring it about.

Oh, the other thing I wanted to mention is, what's so hot about
manufacturing jobs, anyway?  A century ago, two thirds of American workers
were in agriculture; today it's less than two percent.  Manufacturing is
not inherently more important than agriculture; if the money turns out to
be in computers or art or hairdressing or teledildonic prostitution, who
cares who's doing the manufacturing, so long as it gets done?  Do you know
where the Twinkie in your lunch box was made (without looking)?  Do you
know where your lunch box itself was made?  Why should you care?

