The opinion of most Extropians, on the other hand, is that government
itself is the big drain on our pocketbooks, not the actual (microscopic
amount of) money that actually gets into the hands of welfare recipients. 
Once you recognize that the major expenditures of the U.S. government are: 
interest on the national debt (Number 1 with a bullet), entitlement
programs like Social Security and government pensions (which represent
almost $11 trillion in encumbrances -- yes you read that right -- on the
future revenues of Uncle Sammy), and national defense; and when you
recognize that the vast majority of what "we" spend on Health-n-Welfare
programs actually goes to lining the pockets of bureaucrats, rather than
reaching the people it's ostensibly supposed to be helping... then
objection to the whole scheme seems a bit more reasonable.

>Get rid of unions and the
>minimum wage, and we'll get that manufacturing back.

How do you get rid of unions without a state to smash them?  Remember that
in the big strikes of the '30's it was state troopers and national
guardsmen, not private cops, that were busting the heads of the strikers. 
"What's good for GM is good for America", remember?  Union-busting has a
lot more to do with the relations between politicians and their
contributors than it does with big manufacturing concerns taking the law
into their own hands.  Unions are certainly compatible with
anarcho-capitalism... but I doubt that (good) workers would have much need
for a union in such an economy.

The minimum wage, on the other hand, isn't a cause or a cure but a symptom.
 The real disease is the regulatory bodies that keep us from offering
better goods cheaper.  A single example:  Housing.  Consider a house as a
"dwelling machine".  Did you ever notice that your house has the same basic
construction as the ones your great-grandparents lived in?  Wood
construction, studs inside the walls, some kind of covering over the studs,
wooden roofs with shingles, blah blah blah.  Bucky Fuller demonstrated in
the '30's -- THE THIRTIES, f'chrissake -- that better, stronger housing
could be built, using the technologies then being used for airplane
manufacture, at a fraction of the cost of piece-by-piece construction from
outmoded materials.  Nobody built any of his houses because they weren't
"up to code" -- they didn't conform to zoning restrictions and the
construction specifications of housing authorities (who were, naturally, in
the pockets of the people they were supposed to be regulating -- the big
builders and contractors, who were quite happy with building technology the
way it was).  The result is, SIXTY YEARS LATER, you're still living in a
wood- or brick-constructed firetrap with about the same structural
integrity as a damp matchbox.

>Robots create jobs building and maintaining robots.

What, you think robots are going to design themselves?  Robots are DUMB,
