From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: An Interesting Topic

Thank you, Robin Hanson, for bringing up an interesting topic with your
great question!  I'm sorry I said all those nasty things about your idea to
have a Walrasian Whole Universe Auction!  (I stand by them, though :-).

I've come up with a good worry: that the State will do to computer networks
what it did to radio.  I've just read a fascinating history of the subject
written by Carl Watner and published in his newsletter, "The Voluntaryist,"
a few years ago.  Anyone else read it?

Summary: Radio started out much like personal computer networks, through
amateurs inspired by the likes of Gernsback.  Marconi came up with a way
of making a profit even though the British Empire had a law against it:
the "non-intercommunication" rule (no Marconi radios or operators would
communicate with any non-Marconis), which pissed off statists like the
Germans so much that they started holding conferences to call for nationaliza-
tion of the airwaves.  The US Navy supported this.  While a property-rights
system started to evolve in case law based upon the "first in use, first in
right" concept of water rights from the Western USA, it was destroyed by
regulations and the corporatist welfare-warfare State (RCA was formed to
be a coercive monopoly) in the late 1920s and/or early 1930s.

It vindicates theoretical claims that many free-marketeers make about the way
that property rights would develop in airwaves sans interventionist public
policy, but also illustrates how large firms capture legislators, legislation,
and regulators & regulation in order to squelch (pun intended) their
competitors.

My worry is that something analogous will be attempted with computer
networks: declare them public property, along with a right to use them, a
right to get State-approved services, and little if any right to any that
aren't State-approved.  Look to see the private computer networks driven out
by a public "data highway," or incorporated into it, and to see access to it
for computer service providers like programmers controlled by something like
licensure.  Look also to see the content of messages policed and censored
like the FCC does for radio, for "pirate hackers" to be persecuted for
trying to homestead networks, etc.

Net result: State capture of the freest industry left in the world.

