here)? For every situation I can come up with, I can also come up with some
counterexamples to defeat the situation.

On the subject of radio, yes, the government is VERY pervasive there. However,
there are a number of radio transmissions from countries that have no such
power which are being used. There is also the MASSIVE number of shortwave
pirate stations on 7.415MHz which appear almost every single night and appear
to not be shutting down any time soon.

Another example is in the 27MHz band. In the 600KHz+ band directly above the
CB band, there are huge numbers of international talk-arounds and such. These
bands are reached by easily modifying the PLL circuitry of a CB radio and
retuning the filters for the new band. I have talked with people all over the
country already on the lower ("legal") band; my set is just not tuned well
enough (and the antenna is not that great) yet to be able to handle reliable
international communications.

The reason the FCC does not crack down on the "grey band" (as the upper
CB band is referred to) is that it is simply impossible to do so by the shear
numbers of people using it. With shortwave, it is a different matter because
the pirates there use quite a bit more power to get their signal out and they
usually operate as a one-way link (a radio station as such), thus making normal
triangulation techniques very easy.

Two new technologies on the radio horizon include digital radio (with the
powerful spread spectrum technique) and computer controlled repeater stations,
both of which completely defeat the government-imposed monopoly on radio
communications. The better of the two is spread spectrum, which makes a
transmission appear to those who do not have the proper despreading code as
complete background noise, indistinguishable from natural phenomena. In fact,
spread spectrum radios can even cause their output power to appear LOWER than
background noise while still communicating, thus making it impossible to
detect spread spectrum without the proper despreading code.

My expertise is generally more oriented towards radio (does it show?) but the
same could be said for computer networks. I regularly transmit messages to
friends in California with the use of encryption. Even if encryption was banned
from the networks, I could still send them "binary files". And if this was
banned, I could send them plain-text encryption systems based on natural
language synthesis (for a simple example, consider a travesty tree generator
in which the apparently "random" text snippets are actually based on the secret
message which is embedded in them).

On the physical links themselves, they would have to continuously monitor the
phone networks themselves to detect "illegal" modem traffic, a possibility
that I would not pass off as impossible. But what's to stop people from
switching back to the aforementioned radio systems? In fact, I'm in favor of
dumping the phone network entirely and going to wireless short haul networks
on the basis of the fact that the phone companies are going to continue
playing their communications monopoly games, games which I do not wish to
participate in.

